---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    authorSlug={authorSlug}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Security"
    categorySlug="guides"
    subcategorySlug="security"
    headings={headings}
>
  <p class="lead">
    Got an email from my "CEO" last month asking me to wire twenty grand to a new vendor. Urgent. Confidential. Don't
    tell anyone. The email looked perfect. Same signature, same tone, even referenced a real project we'd been
    discussing.
  </p>

  <p>I almost did it.</p>

  <p>
    Caught myself hovering over the reply button. Something felt off. Called my actual boss on her cell. She was like,
    what? No clue what I meant. That email? Phishing. Would've cost me my job and the company twenty thousand bucks.
  </p>

  <p>Remote workers are sitting ducks for this stuff. Let me show you how to avoid that.</p>

  <h2 id="why-easy-targets">Why We're Easy Targets</h2>

  <p>
    Working from home? Great. Until you realize nobody's watching your back. No IT person down the hall to double-check
    a weird email. No <a href="/guides/avoid-miscommunication-remote-teams">coworker</a> glancing over your shoulder
    going "uh, don't click that."
  </p>

  <p>Attackers know this.</p>

  <p>
    They know you're juggling a million <a href="/blog/best-team-communication-tools">Slack messages</a> and emails.
    They know you've never met half your colleagues in
    person. They know you're probably using your personal laptop for some work stuff. They know someone asking you to
    act fast will probably get you to act fast.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="https://www.ic3.gov/Media/PDF/AnnualReport/2023_IC3Report.pdf" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">Phishing
      attacks targeting remote workers jumped over six hundred percent when everyone started working from home</a>
    according to FBI Internet Crime Complaint Center data.
    That number hasn't dropped.
  </p>

  <h2 id="types-of-phishing">The Different Flavors of Phishing</h2>

  <h3>Email Phishing</h3>
  <p>
    The classic. Fake email pretending to be from your bank, Amazon, PayPal, whoever. Goal is getting you to click
    something or grab an attachment. Same old story.
  </p>

  <h3>Spear Phishing</h3>
  <p>
    This one's personal. Attackers dig through your LinkedIn, your social media, maybe data from some breach you forgot
    about. Then they craft something specific. Reference your actual project. Mention your real coworker by name. Way
    more convincing.
  </p>

  <h3>Smishing</h3>
  <p>
    Same thing but via text. "Your package couldn't be delivered." "Suspicious activity on your account." All those
    texts you've been ignoring? Good instinct.
  </p>

  <h3>Vishing</h3>
  <p>
    Phone calls. Someone claiming to be from Microsoft tech support or the IRS or your bank. They use pressure and
    authority. "Your computer has a virus." "You owe <a href="/blog/tax-tips-remote-workers">back taxes</a>." "Your
    account has been compromised."
  </p>

  <h3>Clone Phishing</h3>
  <p>
    Sneaky one. They copy a real email you got before. Same format, same sender name. But they swap out the links. Since
    you recognize the email, you trust it. Bad idea.
  </p>

  <h3>Whaling</h3>
  <p>
    Spear phishing but for the big fish. CEOs, executives, senior leaders. Think fake legal papers, made-up board
    emergencies, secret merger garbage.
  </p>

  <h2 id="spotting-fakes">Spotting the Fakes</h2>

  <h3>Check the Actual Email Address</h3>
  <p>Hover over the sender's name. See what pops up.</p>
  <p>
    <code>support@amaz0n. Com</code>. That's a zero.<br/>
    <code>paypal@secure-payment. Net</code>. That's not PayPal's domain.<br/>
    <code>your-boss@company. Co</code>. Wrong extension.
  </p>
  <p>Quick glance? Looks legit. Exactly what they're counting on.</p>

  <h3>Watch for Panic Buttons</h3>
  <p>
    "Account suspended in twenty-four hours if you don't act."<br/>
    "Act now or face legal action."<br/>
    "Your payment failed. Update now."
  </p>
  <p>Legitimate companies don't usually threaten you into clicking. They'll send follow-ups. They'll let you log in
    through their actual website. Panic is the phisher's best friend.</p>

  <h3>Hover Before You Click</h3>
  <p>
    The text might say login. Microsoft. Com. The actual link? Could go anywhere. Desktop? Hover over it, check the bottom
    left corner. Phone? Hold down on the link to see where it actually goes.
  </p>

  <h3>Generic Greetings</h3>
  <p>
    "Dear Customer" or "Dear User" instead of your actual name. Mass phishing campaigns don't have time to personalize.
    Real companies usually do.
  </p>

  <h3>Bad Writing</h3>
  <p>
    Awkward sentences. Weird spacing. Spelling mistakes a real company would never send. Attackers have gotten better at
    this, but plenty still mess it up.
  </p>

  <h3>Random Attachments</h3>
  <p>
    Didn't expect an invoice? Don't open it. Didn't expect a document from someone? Don't open it.pdfs aren't safe
    either. Learned that one myself.
  </p>

  <h2 id="business-email-compromise">When They Go After Your Company</h2>

  <p>This is where it gets expensive. <a href="https://www.ic3.gov/Media/PDF/AnnualReport/2023_IC3Report.pdf"
                                         target="_blank" rel="nofollow">BEC attacks have cost companies billions</a>
    according to the FBI. Billions with a B.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>CEO Fraud.</strong> Email looks like it's from your CEO. Urgent wire transfer needed. Confidential. Don't
    verify with anyone. The attacker is counting on you not wanting to bother the boss or slow things down.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Invoice Fraud.</strong> Your regular vendor sends updated bank details for the next payment. Except it's not
    your vendor. Money goes to the attacker's account.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Account Compromise.</strong> Someone actually hacks a colleague's email. Then uses it to phish everyone else
    internally. Since it's a real address, people trust it. Why wouldn't they?
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Attorney Impersonation.</strong> Fake lawyers. Urgent confidential matter. Needs immediate action. Legal
    stuff sounds scary, so people comply.
  </p>

  <p>
    How to protect yourself? Verify through a different channel. Call them on a number you already have. Not a number
    from the suspicious email. Real emergencies can handle a two-minute verification call. Scams can't.
  </p>

  <h2 id="already-clicked">Already Clicked? Do This Now</h2>

  <p>Okay. It happened. Don't panic but don't wait either.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>First.</strong> Disconnect from the internet. If something downloaded, this stops it from phoning home or
    spreading.<br/>
    <strong>Second.</strong> Close the browser. Don't enter anything else.<br/>
    <strong>Third.</strong> Run a malware scan. Right now.<br/>
    <strong>Fourth.</strong> Document everything. Screenshot the email. Write down what you clicked, what you entered.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you typed in your password somewhere, change it immediately on the real site. And if you use that password
    anywhere else? Change those too. Turn on <a href="/guides/two-factor-authentication-guide">two-factor
    authentication</a> while you're at it.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you entered credit card or bank info, call your bank. Now. They can freeze things fast. Consider a credit freeze
    if it was really sensitive stuff.
  </p>

  <p>
    And tell your IT team. Yeah, I know it's embarrassing. Tell them anyway. Letting them know means they can warn
    everyone else before it spreads.
  </p>

  <h2 id="getting-better">Getting Better at This</h2>

  <h3>For You Personally</h3>
  <p>
    Be suspicious by default. Unexpected email asking you to do something? Suspicious until proven otherwise.
  </p>
  <p>
    Slow down. Phishing needs you to act fast. Taking thirty extra seconds to verify costs you nothing. Not verifying
    might cost you everything.
  </p>
  <p>Establish verification words with colleagues. Sounds paranoid. Works though.</p>
  <p>Stay current on what scams look like. They evolve. You should too.</p>

  <h3>For Teams and Managers</h3>
  <p>Run fake phishing tests. Regular simulations. Not to catch and punish people. To train them.</p>
  <p>Reward reporting. Someone flags a suspicious email? That's a win. Even if it turns out to be legit.</p>
  <p>Clear procedures for financial requests. Anything over a certain amount gets verified by phone. No exceptions.</p>
  <p>Share examples. When someone catches a phishing attempt, share it with the team. Everyone learns.</p>

  <h2 id="tools-that-help">Stuff That Actually Helps</h2>

  <h3>Email Security</h3>
  <p>Modern spam filters catch a lot. External email warnings flag stuff from outside your organization. Some enterprise
    tools scan links before you click them. Make sure your <a href="/guides/secure-home-wifi-network">home network is
      secure</a> too.</p>

  <h3>Browser Protection</h3>
  <p>Chrome, Firefox, Edge. All have safe browsing that warns about known phishing sites. Check for the padlock on login
    pages.</p>

  <h3>Password Managers</h3>
  <p>
    Here's a trick. Your <a href="/tools/best-password-manager-remote-work">password manager</a> won't auto-fill on fake
    sites because the URL doesn't match. If you go to log
    into "PayPaI.com" instead of "PayPal.com," your password manager just sits there. Accidental phishing protection.
  </p>

  <h3>Two-Factor Authentication</h3>
  <p>
    Password stolen? They still can't log in without that second factor. Authenticator apps beat text messages. SIM
    swapping is a real thing. Phone numbers can be compromised.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reporting">Reporting This Garbage</h2>

  <p>Reporting helps everyone. Gets malicious sites blocked. Gets senders flagged.</p>

  <p>Report to your company's IT. Even if you're not sure. They'd rather have false alarms than missed attacks.</p>

  <p>Report to your email provider. Gmail has "Report phishing" in the menu. Outlook has a "Report" button.</p>

  <p>Report to authorities. In the US, forward to <a href="https://apwg.org/reportphishing/" target="_blank"
                                                     rel="nofollow">reportphishing@apwg.org (Anti-Phishing Working
    Group)</a>. In the UK, report@phishing. Gov. Uk.</p>

  <p>If someone's impersonating a company, most have abuse or security emails. Let them know. Helps protect their other
    customers too.</p>

  <h2 id="faq">Quick Answers</h2>

  <FAQSection faqs={faqData}/>

  <h2>Bottom Line</h2>

  <p>Phishing isn't going away. If anything it's getting smarter. But the basics stay the same.</p>

  <p>Verify before trusting. Slow down when someone's pressuring you. Report anything suspicious.</p>

  <p>
    You're your own first line of defense out there. Build good habits now. Verify weird requests through different
    channels. Use a <a href="/tools/best-password-manager-remote-work">password manager</a>. Turn on <a
      href="/guides/two-factor-authentication-guide">two-factor</a> everywhere. Don't let urgency override your
    judgment.
  </p>

  <p>
    That fake CEO email I almost fell for? It's sitting in a folder now. I pull it out sometimes when training new
    people. "This is what a good phishing email looks like," I tell them. "I almost wired twenty grand."
  </p>

  <p>The embarrassment? Worth sharing. Might save someone else from making the same mistake.</p>

  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
